--- JSONEachRowWithNames output
["id", "name"]
{"id":1,"name":"David"}
{"id":2,"name":"Julie"}
--- JSONEachRowWithNamesAndTypes output
["id", "name"]
["UInt32", "String"]
{"id":1,"name":"David"}
{"id":2,"name":"Julie"}
--- JSONStringsEachRowWithNames output
["id", "name"]
{"id":"1","name":"David"}
{"id":"2","name":"Julie"}
--- JSONStringsEachRowWithNamesAndTypes output
["id", "name"]
["UInt32", "String"]
{"id":"1","name":"David"}
{"id":"2","name":"Julie"}
--- JSONEachRowWithNames input
1	David
2	Julie
--- JSONEachRowWithNamesAndTypes input
1	David
2	Julie
--- JSONStringsEachRowWithNamesAndTypes input
1	David
2	Julie
--- the data rows still carry the names, so a subset of columns can be read
0	David
0	Julie
--- the types from the header are checked against the destination
--- a header row with fewer types than names is rejected
--- schema inference takes the types from the header row
id	UInt32					
arr	Array(UInt64)					
--- without a types row the schema is inferred from the data rows
id	Nullable(Int64)					
arr	Array(Nullable(Int64))					
--- parallel parsing splits object rows on their own boundaries
--- a JSON array of rows cannot contain the header rows
1000	499500	500500
